paragraphs.text:
In general, by the end of the course, you will be able to:
understand the major theories and models of language processing and production;
become familiar with some of the major findings in psycholinguistics;
feel comfortable reading and discussing research articles about psycholinguistics;
have working knowledge of psycholinguistic experimental paradigms;
be familiar with a variety of empirical tools used by psycholinguists to study language comprehension and production; and be able to study linguistic issues from a psycholinguistic perspective.
More specifically,
if students have become familiar with some of the major findings in psycholinguistics, they should be able to discuss the results they obtain in the in-class experiments by completing 5 worksheets.
if students have understood the major theories and models of language processing and production, they should be able to summarize and provide critiques on HW1 and HW2. The former is about speaker normalization, and the latter is about sentence processing.
if students have learned the knowledge of psycholinguistic experimental paradigms, they should be able to provide revisions regarding the experiments they do in class and the experiments in the articles they read, especially those in HW1 and HW2.
if students can study linguistic issues from a psycholinguistic perspective, they should be able to provide future directions for the articles they read in class. They should also be able to complete the final section of the worksheets, HW1 and HW2.

